    {
      "question_id": "q01",
      "source_numbers": ["278"],
      "mode": "single",
      "independent_solution": "From x_r=x_1*q^(r-1), the six exponents are 4, 20, 149, m-1, m+1, and 2k. Thus the complete formula tuple is (x_1*q^4,x_1*q^20,x_1*q^149,x_1*q^(m-1),x_1*q^(m+1),x_1*q^(2k)). All named indices must be valid terms.",
      "correct": ["b"],
      "option_checks": [
        {"option_id": "a", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "Each exponent is one transition too large. With x_1=1,q=2, its first component gives x_5=32, while four actual transitions give 16."},
        {"option_id": "b", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "Subtracting one from each of the six requested indices gives exactly 4,20,149,m-1,m+1,2k, matching every component."},
        {"option_id": "c", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "It doubles each required transition exponent. With x_1=1,q=2, its x_5=2^8=256 instead of 2^4=16."},
        {"option_id": "d", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "Its additive rule is not the geometric recurrence. With x_1=1,q=2, the first expression is 1+4*2=9 instead of 16."}
      ],
      "visible_mathematics": "determinate"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "q02",
      "source_numbers": ["279"],
      "mode": "single",
      "independent_solution": "u_r=256*(1/2)^(r-1). At r=3,5,10 the results are 64,16,1/2. The general term is 256*(1/2)^(n-1), which gives 256 at n=1.",
      "correct": ["d"],
      "option_checks": [
        {"option_id": "a", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "32,8,1/4 are each half the required term; its exponent n also gives 128 rather than 256 at n=1."},
        {"option_id": "b", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "128,32,1 are each twice the required term; its exponent n-2 gives 512 rather than 256 at n=1."},
        {"option_id": "c", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "Its formula uses ratio 2 rather than 1/2. The proposed third term 1024 contradicts 256*(1/2)^2=64."},
        {"option_id": "d", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "All three numeric components are obtained with exponents 2,4,9, and the symbolic exponent n-1 recovers the initial condition."}
      ],
      "visible_mathematics": "determinate"
    },
    {
      "question_id": "q03",
      "source_numbers": ["280"],
      "mode": "single",
      "independent_solution": "Use the four table indices minus one: A=96*(1/2)^6=3/2; B=(1/8)*(-2)^5=-4; C=4*sqrt(2)*(-1/sqrt(2))^8=sqrt(2)/4; D=0.003*(sqrt(10))^6=3. The full ordered result is (3/2,-4,sqrt(2)/4,3).",
      "correct": ["a"],
      "option_checks": [
